**Sensitivity analysis:** Option B overtakes Option A only if the cost of developing the HRM and SPO2 algorithms is truly as difficult as specified. The result is therefore moderately robust, but sensitive to
how heavily the algorithms are to implement.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Decision

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The difficulty of implementing the SPO2 and HRM algorithms on the MCU should be evaluated. If the algorithms are too difficult to implement, then option B should be taken. Otherwise option A is choice to be taken.

Please see the following repo for the analysis of the work required to meet requirement MOE-001-003 with architecture A.
https://github.com/BTyrrell01/ppg-testbed

The repo exists as a testbed for PPG algorithms. The inputs to a test are a set of algorithms, and the data from 2x publicly available PPG datasets. The main question we can answer using the testbed is

"What is the minimum amount of work required to meet requirement MOE-001-003, and will that algorithm fit on the ESP32?"

The secondary questions it helps to answer are:
* What are the pitfalls of each algorithm? Where do they fall apart and where do they excel?
* What data is required to meet MOE-001-003? Do we need multiple wavelengths? Accelerometer data?

The direct outputs of the testbed are the performance of the algorithms across many different parameters. If the bar to meet MOE-001-003 is low, then simple algorithms that port to the ESP32 easily can be developed (by me). If the algorithms are very complex or don't fit on the ESP32, then I will know that we have to go with architecture B.

============================ RESULTS ============================
Baseline algorithms get us almost there, but have dramatic pitfalls.
* Simple peak detect satisfies the requirement, but error goes well above 10bpm when the SNR gets low or motion artifacts corrupt the signal.
* Wavelet transform algorithm with accelerometer data for compensation meets and exceeds the requirements, while taking 81KB of RAM on the ESP32 (Not too shabby!)

file:///Users/ben/Projects/PPG_algorithm/results/dalia_dev_baselines/report.html

We are going with option A! If implementing the Wavelet transform + accelerometer data is too difficult we can just use the baseline FFT + accelerometer compensation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
